{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run1:labor:redteam","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:e5aa709c6cc6d476ac42c8a0e60988412df3dbf95116d0932487e31ebfeed16d","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":1.2},{"probability":0.5,"value":2.1},{"probability":0.9,"value":3.1}]},"questionHash":"sha256:e1b53adbdbe1bb1ccef339035b6376b49668b9341050c684f480d5b347e316f6","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq3","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"Wave 1 produced 1.8 / 1.9 / 1.9 for P50 and 1.0-1.1 / 2.8 for the tails — three independent lenses landing inside 0.1pp. That is not triangulation, it is a shared input. The shared input is the OECD leg at 1.1-1.2% and the China leg at 2.8-3.0%, and both are wrong in the same direction.\n\nTHE STRONGEST COHERENT WORLD IN WHICH THE WAVE-1 MODAL VIEW IS SIMPLY WRONG, probability ~35%: \"Care-Demand Deflation + Reported-Growth Floor + Scarcity Mechanization.\" Morbidity compression (2A's own 41-country, N~1M capacity result) cuts the 2036 care-FTE requirement 20-35% below Panelist 2's headcount derivation; the labour-scarcity wage signal induces genuine capital deepening rather than rationing (the Habakkuk mechanism, and Acemoglu-Restrepo 2017 find NO negative cross-country relation between aging and GDP-per-capita growth 1990-2015, if anything positive, with aging countries adopting MORE robots — a result none of the three panelists cites and which is the single best available falsification of the 2A counterfactual's propagation into a FORECAST); the healthy-aging capacity gain lands in the Solow residual and prints as TFP; and reported Chinese growth holds its institutional floor. In that world combined growth is 2.5-2.9%, CQ6-A resolves, and Wave 1's 11-13 for A is a factor-of-two error.\n\nMY BUILD-UP. Weights: OECD-38 ~ $78-80T 2025 PPP, China ~ $40T, combined ~ $118-120T, so China ~ 0.34 / OECD ~ 0.66. Panelist 1 used 0.30/0.70, which biased his composite down ~7bp on his own figures; Panelists 2-3 used 0.35-0.36.\nCHINA LEG. All three take 2.8-3.0%. But the question resolves on NBS-REPORTED real GDP as reprinted by the IMF. Panelist 3 identified the reported-versus-deflator-consistent wedge (+1 to +1.5pp, +0.35 to +0.55pp on the composite) and then used 3.0% anyway, applying the wedge to a \"true growth\" baseline he never wrote down — an unresolved double standard that silently discards his own largest swing factor. Reference class for REPORTED Chinese growth: no sub-4% print in 35 years except 2020 and 2022, and 15th/16th Five-Year-Plan targets will be set around 4.5-5% and then ~4%. The institutional floor is a property of the resolving series, not of the economy. China 2032-36 reported: P50 3.6%, P10 2.0%, P90 4.8%.\nOECD LEG. 2A gives per-capita 1.0 -> 0.6%/yr \"absent productivity offsets\". Population: OECD-38 includes the US, Canada, Australia and the UK; aggregate population growth has run ~0.4-0.5%/yr on migration and tightens from here — use +0.25%. Productivity offset: Wave 1 used +0.15 to +0.28pp. I use +0.35pp, for two reasons Wave 1 itself supplied and then refused to propagate. First, Panelist 3's attribution point: growth accounting measures labour as bodies and hours with at best an education adjustment, so the \"70 in 2022 = 53 in 2000\" capacity gain is an unmeasured labour-quality improvement that lands in the residual and is PUBLISHED as TFP — he flagged this as a coupling tension and then left it out of his own CQ3. Second, Panelist 1 established the labour-scarcity adoption pull (and would raise AI-Q2 to 38-45%) and then declared, without argument, that \"this upward revision does NOT propagate to AI-Q1 or to my CQ3.\" That is the drag/pull asymmetry in its purest form: aging is allowed to raise the QUANTITY of adoption and forbidden from raising its measured PRODUCT. Retirement-age extension adds ~+0.10pp of hours. Net against Panelist 2's Baumol reallocation drag of -0.15 to -0.20pp/yr, which I halve because the hours shift into Q is itself endogenous to the care-demand deflation above. OECD-38 P50 = 1.30%.\nCOMPOSITE P50 = 0.66(1.30) + 0.34(3.60) = 0.858 + 1.224 = 2.08 -> 2.1%.\nP10 = 1.2%: an OECD recession year inside the window plus a Chinese property/fiscal hard landing, but the left tail is FATTER-TAILED-BUT-SHORTER than Wave 1's because a sub-1.5% composite requires a break in the reported-growth regime, not merely a bad economy.\nP90 = 3.1%: OECD 1.9% with the residual carrying the capacity gain, reported China 4.6%, and a 2032-36 migration surge relieving the care constraint.\nI accept the locked 2A healthy-aging identity on Panelist 3's NET reading (-1.1pp/yr is net of the +0.4pp) and do not double-count it; my upward revision comes from the measurement and reported-series channels, not from re-adding that term.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:51.421Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"NBS reports a calendar-year real GDP growth rate below 3.5% for any year, absent a declared pandemic or war shock, as reprinted in the IMF WEO.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies the reported-series institutional floor, my single largest CQ3 driver. CQ3 P50 falls from 2.1% to ~1.8% and P90 to ~2.8%; CQ6-A falls from 22 to ~13 and D rises from 7 to ~12; CQ4 P50 falls ~15bp on the lower-g channel. This is the cleanest single falsifier of my whole payload.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"US BLS data (OES median hourly by industry, or ECI by occupation group) show healthcare-support plus construction-and-extraction real median hourly wages cumulatively BELOW information plus finance plus professional-and-business-services, measured from 2025, for two consecutive annual vintages.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies my claim that the 